{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1787902386631530001,"compiled_state":{"alias":"wounded-resident-bushbaby","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":2,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"ce326f1cfaa10b3d469719dfd7fa4637368c4d0b5ddb2a2e5c7d3b1410a29558","event_uuid":"5572f176-751b-49d9-9239-c3ca13447eff","position":{"commit_sha":"8b4d151a5041b53444ca31c00cb72a6869698a51","position":"1787879713995019768-5572f176-751b-49d9-9239-c3ca13447eff"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"e3c21aa6542a4d44b60c2bfb5f5317a4214a47710cf7f40fd502318a521e4b21","event_uuid":"7c61e654-62bb-48a5-802a-cd811e8a8c16","position":{"commit_sha":"8a4a014256c0ecffcc8e5d6a752d97624c80a19f","position":"1787879736466573797-7c61e654-62bb-48a5-802a-cd811e8a8c16"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1787879713995019768,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[{"link_uuid":"7c61e654-62bb-48a5-802a-cd811e8a8c16","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"f643-2ac4-ecc2-419b"}],"description":"## Summary\n\nDiscovered while root-causing `superior-trifling-dunlin` (f643). That bug proved, at runtime,\nthat the python `mcp` SDK invokes a **sync** `@mcp.tool` function DIRECTLY inside the ASGI\nrequest coroutine (`mcp/server/fastmcp/utilities/func_metadata.py:96` — `return\nfn(**arguments_parsed_dict)`, no thread offload). A sync tool body therefore executes on the\n**event-loop thread**, not on an `anyio.to_thread` worker.\n\nf643 fixed the consequence for the LLM drive. This ticket is about a SECOND consumer of the same\nfalse premise that f643 deliberately did NOT touch: the graceful-drain / zero-downtime-deploy\ncontract.\n\n## The concern\n\n`src/rebar/_mcp_health.py:259-278` `make_sigterm_handler` waits for the `InFlightGauge` to drain\nto 0 before the process exits, so a deploy never kills an in-flight certified op. That is\ncoherent only if the op body runs on a *different* thread from the one servicing the signal. It\ndoes not: `review_plan` / `verify_completion` / `review_code` / `scan_spec` are all sync `def`\n(`src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py:87, :123, :151, :205`), so a 15-20 minute certified op occupies the loop\nthread for its whole duration.\n\nThe contract this puts at risk is specified in:\n- `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md:71`, `:79-80`, `:91`, `:170` (\"The deploy path never kills an\n  in-flight certified op\")\n- `docs/adr/0079-autodeploy-on-box-timer.md:85`, `:92-93`\n\n## NOT YET PROVEN — this is a lead, not a confirmed defect\n\nI did not run the experiment, and the answer is genuinely uncertain in both directions:\n\n- CPython runs signal handlers on the **main** thread between bytecodes, and after f643 the main\n  thread blocks in `concurrent.futures.Future.result()` (a `threading.Condition` wait), which IS\n  interruptible. So f643 may have INCIDENTALLY repaired the drain.\n- Before f643 the main thread was inside `run_until_complete`, and the drain may already have\n  been a no-op in exactly the case it exists for.\n\nSomeone should settle it with an actual experiment (send SIGTERM to a server with a certified op\nin flight, observe whether the handler runs and whether the gauge drains) rather than reasoning\nabout it, and then either close this as working-as-designed or fix it.\n\n## Related smell (same file)\n\n`src/rebar/_mcp_health.py:107` — `instrument_certified_tools` explicitly SKIPS `is_async` tools.\nIf the certified tools are ever converted to `async def` (a plausible remedy for the loop\nblocking), the in-flight gauge silently stops counting them and the drain contract fails open\nwith no signal. Worth a guard either way.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An experiment establishes whether SIGTERM is serviced, and the gauge drains, while a\n      certified op is in flight — run BOTH against the f643 fix and (for comparison) with the\n      guard disabled, so the incidental-repair question is answered rather than assumed.\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md` and `docs/adr/0079-autodeploy-on-box-timer.md` state the\n      real threading model of a sync MCP tool body.\n- [ ] `_mcp_health.py:107`'s silent `is_async` skip either counts async tools or fails loudly.\n","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":["mcp","deploy","asyncio"],"ticket_id":"b580-98b6-d9b7-4817","ticket_type":"bug","title":"The MCP graceful-drain contract assumes certified tool bodies run off the loop thread; they do not","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["5572f176-751b-49d9-9239-c3ca13447eff","7c61e654-62bb-48a5-802a-cd811e8a8c16"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787902386631530001,"uuid":"6afa992b-32bc-4091-ace2-2993e4a3a6e4"}